<<<
* stepping |

hyperbaton (Gram.);

Transposition of words out over'. of normal order, as in Browning's |} title Wanting Is—What?, or in ji Shakspere's That whiter skin of hers than snow. |
===
*hyperbaton* (Gram.); 'stepping over'. Transposition of words out of normal order, as in Browning's title *Wanting Is—What?*, or in Shakspere's *That whiter skin of hers than snow.*
>>>

<<<
hyperbole (Rhet.); ' over-shooting'. | Use of exaggerated terms for the | sake not of deception, but oi' emphasis, as when infinite is used for great, or 'a thousand apologies ` | for an apology. '|
===
*hyperbole* (Rhet.); 'over-shooting'. Use of exaggerated terms for the sake not of deception, but of emphasis, as when *infinite* is used for *great*, or 'a thousand apologies' for an apology.
>>>

<<<
hypermetre (adj. -trical) (Pros.): t over-measure °. In Latin verse, a | syllable at the end of a line after the | metre is complete, to be elided | before a vowel beginning the next | line; a rarity.
===
*hypermetre* (adj. *-trical*) (Pros.); 'over-measure'. In Latin verse, a syllable at the end of a line after the metre is complete, to be elided before a vowel beginning the next line; a rarity.
>>>

<<<
Sometimes applied | in English to lines having a feminine instead of the normal masculine | ending; no rarity in blank verse. |
===
Sometimes applied in English to lines having a feminine instead of the normal masculine ending; no rarity in blank verse.
>>>

<<<
hypocorisma (adj. -ristic) (Rhet.) `| * child’s prattle °. Use of pet names, | nursery words, or diminutives, oF a word of these kinds, either simply- | as Molly for Mary, patball for lawn tennis, hanky for handkerchief, &c.,
===
*hypocorisma* (adj. *-ristic*) (Rhet.); 'child's prattle'. Use of pet names, nursery words, or diminutives, or a word of these kinds, either simply, as *Molly* for Mary, *patball* for lawn tennis, *hanky* for handkerchief, &c.,
>>>

